-- AWS Certified Security - Specialty (SCS-C02).
-- Domains: 1 Threat Detection & IR (14%), 2 Security Logging & Monitoring (18%),
-- 3 Infrastructure Security (20%), 4 IAM (16%), 5 Data Protection (18%),
-- 6 Management & Security Governance (14%).
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- Domain 1: Threat Detection & Incident Response
('sc-f-001','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What is Amazon GuardDuty?$$,$$A managed threat-detection service that analyzes CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs with ML/threat intel to flag reconnaissance, compromised instances, and account anomalies - no agents required.$$),
('sc-f-002','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What is Amazon Detective used for?$$,$$Investigating and visualizing the root cause of security findings by building linked graphs from CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and GuardDuty - it analyzes, it doesn''t detect.$$),
('sc-f-003','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What does AWS Security Hub provide?$$,$$A central aggregation of findings from GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, and partner tools, with automated security-standard checks (CIS, PCI, AWS FSBP) and a unified dashboard.$$),
('sc-f-004','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$How do you automate response to a GuardDuty finding?$$,$$An EventBridge rule matches the finding and triggers a target - Lambda (isolate instance, revoke keys), SNS notification, or an SSM Automation/SOAR playbook.$$),
('sc-f-005','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$What are the first steps to contain a compromised EC2 instance?$$,$$Isolate it with a restrictive security group, preserve evidence (snapshot the EBS volume, capture memory), detach from Auto Scaling, and rotate any exposed credentials.$$),
('sc-f-006','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What does Amazon Inspector assess?$$,$$Automated, continuous vulnerability scanning of EC2 instances, container images in ECR, and Lambda functions - reporting CVEs and network reachability with risk scores.$$),
('sc-f-007','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$If an IAM access key is leaked publicly, what should you do FIRST?$$,$$Deactivate/delete the key immediately, then review CloudTrail for unauthorized use, rotate credentials, and revoke any active sessions. Don''t just create a new key alongside it.$$),
('sc-f-008','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$How does GuardDuty detect cryptomining or C2 activity?$$,$$It inspects VPC Flow Logs and DNS logs against threat intelligence and behavioral baselines, flagging communication with known-bad IPs/domains and anomalous outbound traffic.$$),
-- Domain 2: Security Logging & Monitoring
('sc-f-009','awsscs','core',2,'2.1',$$What does AWS CloudTrail record?$$,$$API activity across the account - who did what, when, from where. Management events by default; data events (S3 object-level, Lambda invokes) optionally. Essential for audit and forensics.$$),
('sc-f-010','awsscs','core',2,'2.1',$$How do you protect CloudTrail logs from tampering?$$,$$Deliver to a dedicated S3 bucket in a separate logging account, enable log file validation (digest files), apply MFA delete/Object Lock, and restrict bucket access with SCPs.$$),
('sc-f-011','awsscs','core',2,'2.2',$$What is Amazon CloudWatch Logs used for in security?$$,$$Centralizing application/OS/VPC logs, running metric filters to alarm on patterns (e.g., root login, failed auth), and feeding dashboards and automated responses.$$),
('sc-f-012','awsscs','core',2,'2.1',$$What are VPC Flow Logs?$$,$$Records of IP traffic metadata (source, destination, ports, bytes, accept/reject) for a VPC, subnet, or ENI - used to detect anomalies, troubleshoot, and feed GuardDuty.$$),
('sc-f-013','awsscs','core',2,'2.2',$$What does AWS Config do for security?$$,$$Records resource configuration state and changes over time, evaluates resources against Config Rules (e.g., "no public S3"), and supports remediation - continuous compliance and drift detection.$$),
('sc-f-014','awsscs','core',2,'2.2',$$How can you get alerted when someone logs in as root?$$,$$A CloudWatch Logs metric filter on the CloudTrail log group matching root console sign-in, tied to a CloudWatch alarm and SNS notification (a CIS benchmark control).$$),
('sc-f-015','awsscs','core',2,'2.1',$$What is a CloudTrail organization trail?$$,$$A single trail that logs API activity for all accounts in an AWS Organization into a central bucket - member accounts cannot disable or modify it.$$),
('sc-f-016','awsscs','core',2,'2.2',$$How do you centralize logs across many accounts?$$,$$Aggregate CloudTrail, Config, and CloudWatch Logs into a dedicated logging account''s S3 bucket / log destination, with cross-account permissions and SCP protection.$$),
('sc-f-017','awsscs','core',2,'2.1',$$What is log file integrity validation in CloudTrail?$$,$$CloudTrail creates signed digest files so you can cryptographically verify that delivered log files were not modified or deleted after delivery.$$),
-- Domain 3: Infrastructure Security
('sc-f-018','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What is AWS Network Firewall?$$,$$A managed, stateful VPC firewall supporting Suricata-compatible IPS rules, domain filtering, and deep packet inspection at the VPC level - beyond security groups and NACLs.$$),
('sc-f-019','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$Security group vs NACL vs AWS Network Firewall - layering?$$,$$Security groups (stateful, instance), NACLs (stateless, subnet), and Network Firewall (stateful IPS/domain filtering, VPC) form defense in depth at different scopes.$$),
('sc-f-020','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$What does AWS WAF protect and where is it deployed?$$,$$Layer 7 protection (SQLi, XSS, rate limiting, bot control) attached to CloudFront, ALB, API Gateway, or AppSync via managed and custom rule groups.$$),
('sc-f-021','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$How do you privately connect to AWS services without internet exposure?$$,$$VPC endpoints: Gateway endpoints for S3/DynamoDB, Interface endpoints (PrivateLink) for most others - traffic stays on the AWS network.$$),
('sc-f-022','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What is an endpoint policy on a VPC endpoint?$$,$$A resource policy on the endpoint restricting which principals and API actions can pass through it - e.g., only allow access to specific S3 buckets.$$),
('sc-f-023','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$What is AWS Shield Advanced?$$,$$Paid DDoS protection adding Layer 3/4/7 mitigations, cost protection for scaling during attacks, WAF integration, and 24/7 DDoS Response Team access.$$),
('sc-f-024','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a bastion/jump host and a modern alternative?$$,$$A hardened host for admin access to private instances. The modern alternative is Systems Manager Session Manager - no open inbound ports, no bastion, full session logging.$$),
('sc-f-025','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$Why prefer SSM Session Manager over SSH bastions?$$,$$No inbound ports or key pairs to manage, access controlled by IAM, and every session is logged to CloudTrail/S3 - smaller attack surface and better audit.$$),
('sc-f-026','awsscs','core',3,'3.2',$$How does AWS Firewall Manager help at scale?$$,$$It centrally configures and enforces WAF rules, Shield Advanced, security groups, and Network Firewall policies across all accounts in an Organization.$$),
('sc-f-027','awsscs','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a NAT Gateway''s security role?$$,$$It lets private-subnet instances make outbound internet connections (patches, APIs) without allowing inbound connections from the internet.$$),
-- Domain 4: Identity & Access Management
('sc-f-028','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$How does IAM policy evaluation resolve allow vs deny?$$,$$Default deny; an explicit Allow grants access; an explicit Deny always wins. SCPs, permission boundaries, session policies, and resource policies all narrow the effective permissions.$$),
('sc-f-029','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What is an IAM permission boundary?$$,$$A managed policy that sets the MAXIMUM permissions an IAM user/role can have - it caps, but doesn''t grant. Useful for safely delegating role creation.$$),
('sc-f-030','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$SCP vs permission boundary vs IAM policy?$$,$$SCP caps an account/OU''s permissions; permission boundary caps a single identity''s; the identity policy grants. Effective access is the intersection of all applicable caps and grants.$$),
('sc-f-031','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$What is AWS IAM Identity Center (formerly SSO)?$$,$$Central workforce access to multiple AWS accounts and apps with single sign-on, permission sets, and federation to an external IdP (Okta, Entra ID, AD).$$),
('sc-f-032','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$How do you enable federated access with an external identity provider?$$,$$Configure SAML 2.0 or OIDC federation (or IAM Identity Center) so users authenticate at the IdP and assume IAM roles via STS - no IAM users needed.$$),
('sc-f-033','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$What is an STS AssumeRole and when is it used?$$,$$It returns temporary credentials for a role - used for cross-account access, federation, and giving workloads scoped, expiring permissions instead of long-term keys.$$),
('sc-f-034','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$What is a resource-based policy and a key example?$$,$$A policy attached to a resource (S3 bucket policy, KMS key policy, SQS/SNS/Lambda) that grants access to principals, including cross-account, without them assuming a role.$$),
('sc-f-035','awsscs','core',4,'4.1',$$Why use IAM Access Analyzer?$$,$$It identifies resources shared with external entities (public/cross-account access) and can validate/generate least-privilege policies from CloudTrail activity.$$),
('sc-f-036','awsscs','core',4,'4.2',$$What is an IAM condition key and give a security example?$$,$$A policy condition constraining when access applies - e.g., aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent to require MFA, or aws:SourceIp to restrict to office ranges.$$),
-- Domain 5: Data Protection
('sc-f-037','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$What is the difference between a KMS AWS-managed key and a customer-managed key?$$,$$AWS-managed keys are created/controlled by services with limited configurability; customer-managed keys give you control over rotation, key policy, grants, and deletion.$$),
('sc-f-038','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$What is a KMS key policy vs a grant?$$,$$The key policy is the primary resource policy governing who can use/manage the key; grants are temporary, programmatic delegations of specific key operations to a principal.$$),
('sc-f-039','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$What does automatic key rotation in KMS do?$$,$$For customer-managed keys, it generates new backing key material yearly while keeping the key ID and old material to decrypt existing data - transparent to applications.$$),
('sc-f-040','awsscs','core',5,'5.2',$$What is Amazon Macie?$$,$$A managed service using ML to discover, classify, and protect sensitive data (PII, credentials) in S3, alerting on exposed or unusually accessed sensitive objects.$$),
('sc-f-041','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$How do you enforce that an S3 bucket rejects unencrypted uploads?$$,$$A bucket policy denying s3:PutObject when the encryption header is absent/incorrect, plus default encryption and S3 Block Public Access.$$),
('sc-f-042','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$What is envelope encryption and why does KMS use it?$$,$$Data is encrypted with a data key; the data key is encrypted by the KMS key. It avoids sending large data to KMS and limits exposure of the master key while scaling encryption.$$),
('sc-f-043','awsscs','core',5,'5.2',$$What is S3 Block Public Access?$$,$$Account- and bucket-level settings that override any policy/ACL granting public access - the safety net against accidental public buckets.$$),
('sc-f-044','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$What is AWS CloudHSM and when is it needed?$$,$$A dedicated single-tenant hardware security module for full control of keys and FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance - used when regulations forbid multi-tenant KMS.$$),
('sc-f-045','awsscs','core',5,'5.2',$$How do you protect data in transit within AWS?$$,$$Enforce TLS (ACM certs on ELB/CloudFront/API Gateway), deny non-HTTPS via policy (aws:SecureTransport), and use VPC endpoints/PrivateLink to keep traffic off the internet.$$),
('sc-f-046','awsscs','core',5,'5.1',$$What is a KMS key grant used for in cross-service access?$$,$$It lets a service (e.g., an EBS volume, Lambda) use a KMS key for specific operations temporarily without modifying the key policy - least-privilege, revocable.$$),
-- Domain 6: Management & Security Governance
('sc-f-047','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What are AWS Organizations SCPs for governance?$$,$$Org-wide guardrails that cap what accounts can do (e.g., deny disabling CloudTrail, restrict regions) - preventive controls that even account admins can''t override.$$),
('sc-f-048','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What is AWS Control Tower?$$,$$A service that sets up and governs a secure multi-account landing zone with guardrails (SCPs + Config rules), a log archive account, and an audit account by default.$$),
('sc-f-049','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What does AWS Config conformance pack provide?$$,$$A packaged collection of Config rules and remediation actions mapped to a compliance framework, deployable across accounts for continuous governance.$$),
('sc-f-050','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$How do you restrict which regions accounts can use?$$,$$An SCP with a deny on all actions except approved services when aws:RequestedRegion is not in an allowed list - enforced org-wide.$$),
('sc-f-051','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$What is AWS Trusted Advisor''s security role?$$,$$It checks the account against best practices - exposed access keys, open security groups, MFA on root, public snapshots - and flags remediation.$$),
('sc-f-052','awsscs','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the value of a multi-account strategy for security?$$,$$Blast-radius isolation, separation of duties (dedicated logging/audit/security accounts), per-account guardrails, and clearer cost/compliance boundaries.$$),
('sc-f-053','awsscs','core',6,'6.2',$$How does Security Hub support governance?$$,$$It continuously runs automated compliance checks against standards (CIS, PCI DSS, AWS FSBP) and aggregates findings org-wide with severity and remediation guidance.$$),
-- acronyms
('sc-a-001','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.1',$$IR$$,$$Incident Response - detecting, containing, and recovering from security events.$$),
('sc-a-002','awsscs','acronym',2,'2.1',$$VPC Flow Logs$$,$$Records of IP traffic metadata for a VPC/subnet/ENI, used for anomaly detection.$$),
('sc-a-003','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.1',$$SCP$$,$$Service Control Policy - Organizations guardrail capping account permissions.$$),
('sc-a-004','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.1',$$STS$$,$$Security Token Service - issues temporary credentials for assumed roles.$$),
('sc-a-005','awsscs','acronym',5,'5.1',$$KMS$$,$$Key Management Service - managed encryption key creation and control.$$),
('sc-a-006','awsscs','acronym',5,'5.1',$$CMK$$,$$Customer Master Key (now "KMS key") - the primary encryption key resource in KMS.$$),
('sc-a-007','awsscs','acronym',5,'5.1',$$HSM$$,$$Hardware Security Module - dedicated tamper-resistant key hardware (CloudHSM).$$),
('sc-a-008','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.2',$$WAF$$,$$Web Application Firewall - Layer 7 filtering for web apps.$$),
('sc-a-009','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.1',$$SG$$,$$Security Group - stateful instance-level virtual firewall.$$),
('sc-a-010','awsscs','acronym',3,'3.1',$$NACL$$,$$Network Access Control List - stateless subnet-level allow/deny filter.$$),
('sc-a-011','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.1',$$FSBP$$,$$Foundational Security Best Practices - AWS Security Hub standard.$$),
('sc-a-012','awsscs','acronym',2,'2.2',$$SSM$$,$$Systems Manager - management incl. Session Manager and Automation.$$),
('sc-a-013','awsscs','acronym',6,'6.1',$$OU$$,$$Organizational Unit - a grouping of accounts in AWS Organizations.$$),
('sc-a-014','awsscs','acronym',5,'5.1',$$FIPS$$,$$Federal Information Processing Standards - crypto compliance (e.g., 140-2/3).$$),
('sc-a-015','awsscs','acronym',4,'4.2',$$IdP$$,$$Identity Provider - external directory used for federated authentication.$$),
-- feynman
('sc-y-001','awsscs','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain how GuardDuty, Security Hub, and Detective work together.$$,$$Model answer: They cover detect, aggregate, and investigate. GuardDuty is the detector - it watches CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS with threat intel and flags suspicious behavior like a compromised instance. Security Hub is the aggregator - it collects GuardDuty's findings plus Inspector, Macie, and compliance-standard checks into one prioritized dashboard. Detective is the investigator - when you have a finding, it builds linked graphs of the related activity so you can trace root cause and scope. Detect, centralize, investigate.$$),
('sc-y-002','awsscs','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain how SCPs, permission boundaries, and IAM policies combine to determine effective permissions.$$,$$Model answer: Think of it as nested ceilings and one grant. The SCP is the account-wide ceiling - nothing in the account can exceed it. The permission boundary is a per-identity ceiling on top of that. The IAM identity policy is what actually grants permissions. Your effective access is the intersection: an action is allowed only if the identity policy grants it AND it's under the permission boundary AND it's under the SCP - and any explicit Deny anywhere overrides everything. Grants add up, but every ceiling can only take away.$$),
('sc-y-003','awsscs','feynman',5,'5.1',$$Explain envelope encryption and why KMS doesn't just encrypt your data directly.$$,$$Model answer: KMS keys never leave the service, and sending gigabytes of data to KMS to encrypt would be slow and expose the master key to huge data flows. So instead KMS generates a data key, hands you a plaintext copy to encrypt your data locally, plus an encrypted copy of that data key. You store the encrypted data key next to the ciphertext and throw away the plaintext key. To decrypt, you send the encrypted data key back to KMS, it returns the plaintext key, and you decrypt locally. The master key stays inside KMS, and encryption scales to any data size.$$),
('sc-y-004','awsscs','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain why SSM Session Manager is more secure than a traditional SSH bastion host.$$,$$Model answer: A bastion needs an open inbound SSH port and SSH keys to manage - that's attack surface and secrets that can leak. Session Manager flips it: the instance makes an outbound connection to Systems Manager, so there are no inbound ports to open and no SSH keys. Access is controlled entirely by IAM policies, and every command in every session is logged to CloudTrail and S3. You get a smaller attack surface, no key management, and complete auditability - none of which a classic bastion gives you cleanly.$$),
('sc-y-005','awsscs','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain how you'd protect CloudTrail logs so an attacker who compromises an account can't erase their tracks.$$,$$Model answer: The trick is to get the logs out of the compromised account's reach. Send CloudTrail to an S3 bucket in a separate, locked-down logging account that the workload accounts have no delete access to. Enable log file validation so any tampering is cryptographically detectable, turn on S3 Object Lock/MFA delete so logs are immutable, and use an organization trail plus SCPs so member accounts can't disable logging at all. Even if an attacker gets admin in one account, the evidence lives somewhere they can't touch and can't silently alter.$$);
